  "query_preview": "..\n(Friday through Tuesday)\nIssued at 250 PM MDT Wed Sep 23 2026\n\nSouthwest flow will begin to take over on Friday, leading to a \ndrying trend of the atmosphere from southwest to northeast. There \nwill still be enough moisture around though for scattered showers \nand storms Friday afternoon and evening. Furthermore, jet dynamics \nbecome more favorable as the cyclonic jet streak strengthens as it \nmoves into the high plains of eastern NM/west Texas. Rain chances \nwill focus over northern and eastern New Mexico Friday, with lower \nchances across the west. Most likely rainfall amounts remain below \n1.5\" areawide for Friday, but 90th percentile guidance is showing \npotential for 2-3\" in the east-central plains where soil moisture \nshould be very high by Friday. Dry air finally takes over on \nSaturday, which will be a pleasant day with partly cloudy skies and \nnear-average temperatures. \n\nThe sub-tropical jet stream continues to get more active early next \nweek, seeing the return of high rain chances. The moisture from now \nmajor Hurricane Polo will likely get caught up in the jet stream and \nadvected over the desert southwest during this period, although its \nnot yet clear the exact trajectory of this moisture. Nonetheless, \nLREF mean PWATs rise above the 90th percentile areawide again Monday \nand Tuesday of next week. 10th percentile rainfall amounts have \nalmost no precipitation over New Mexico during this period, \nsuggesting that the return of deep, subtropical moisture is not a \nguarantee just yet. Trends in the progression of the trough digging \ninto the Mojave Desert will be monitored closely as this will \ndetermine where moisture gets pushed.",
